{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791309784,"nonce":"f7+s5LLOFm0z1VpaVbeJYiUWxH0=","message":"After 57b6a9167470ed4cbc796d4e2ba40282a1a12e7b, the fresh-slab threading cost is gone and the remaining allocation boundary is visible in _idx: two mixed integer comparisons, zb_i64_arith_any(2, y, width) producing a box, zb_any_arith_i64(0, that_box, x) producing another box, then a boxed return consumed by scalar array indexing. The optimized SOR region still calls _idx six times per grid update. Its width and height fields remain Object; this is real dynamic arithmetic and cannot be replaced by raw i64 loads without guards.\n\nThe post-allocator profile has about 21% of samples in zyntax_alloc and about 15% in number_i64; collection is about 1%. d5716d49118426b4c82092ebcff55810ed222512d5ec3d89d7b74dc97f1d5710 tests bounded inlining of the number conversion, but does not remove the boxed multiply/add/return chain.\n\nThe larger next lever remains guarded numeric-field specialization or elimination of boxed results across the _idx call boundary. Preserve Object storage, mutations, subclass/custom-operation behavior and exception order. In particular, moving an unbox into a callee before the caller checks its pending-exception flag can replace the original exception, so a scalar-return clone must preserve that control flow. Merely increasing global inliner budgets is not justified by this profile.","files":null}]}